:title("PHP (subset) to LLVM IR compiler") ;
:description("Compiles a PHP subset to an LLVM IR module and runs it with the built-in
IR interpreter and the MetaJS handle runtime (llvm.RunJS).

A file starts in HTML MODE: text outside '<?php ... ?>' is literal output (one print
of a constant string), '?>' also stands in for a missing ';', the newline right after
a close tag is swallowed and '<?= e ?>' is shorthand for echo.

The subset matches php-interpreter.abnf: the '<?php' open tag, dollar-sigil variables
(create on first assignment, plus += -= *= /= %= .=), integers, double/single quoted
strings with '$var' / '{$expr}' interpolation, true/false/null, arithmetic and comparison,
the '.' concatenation operator, && || ! and the words and/or, the ternary, prefix/postfix
++ --, if/elseif/else, while, for (;;), foreach ($a as $v) / ($a as $k => $v), functions
with return and DEFAULT parameter values, arrow functions fn($x) => e, array
destructuring (nested, keyed and with holes, in an assignment and in foreach),
closures function($x) use ($y) {...}, class C { public function m() {...} }
with $this->prop / ->method() / new C(...), indexed and associative arrays ([...] and
array(...)), $a[] append, the builtins count/strlen/in_array/array_keys/array_values/
array_push/abs/max/min, and echo/print.

PHP's VALUE MODEL is the one php-interpreter.abnf documents, and the two must agree:
an int is a plain number, a float is BOXED (so 3.0 is not identical to 3 and match(3.0)
picks the float arm), a string is byte based, and an array is ONE ordered key/value map
with a next-automatic-index, so === compares the keys and their order while == does not.
Truthiness is PHP's (false, 0, 0.0, the empty string, the string zero, the empty array
and null are falsy), == is PHP 8 loose equality and the whole operator ladder, the casts
and count/strlen/in_array/abs/max/min go through the js_ph* externals (abnf/jsrtphp.go),
each the twin of the like-named function in the interpreter.

Classes carry class CONSTANTS and STATIC properties in two prefixed key spaces on the
descriptor, both resolved by walking the inheritance chain - so a subclass shares its
parent's single static storage. self:: and static:: live in the CALL FRAME, not in
globals, so the scope-chain snapshot a try restores covers them; static:: is the runtime
class of this, or the class a C::m() call named (late static binding).

Locals, functions, methods and closures lower to the MetaJS handle runtime: scopes via
js_scope_new / js_pyset_var (PHP create-on-assign) / js_scope_get, closures via js_closure
+ js_call, objects/properties via js_obj_new / js_get / js_set, method dispatch via
js_mcall (instances carry a __class descriptor; new C() runs __construct with the instance
bound to $this), arrays via js_pharr / js_phset / js_phpush, indexing via js_phget,
foreach via js_range_len / js_range_key / js_range_val over that same ordered map.
'.' concatenation coerces via js_phcat; && || coerce to booleans with js_phbool. The whole program becomes phpmain(env,
args); a self checking program ends with exit(failures). The same subset is interpreted
directly by php-interpreter.abnf, and the two must agree.") ;


// --- main rules

// Shared token productions (languages/lib): identifiers, integers, booleans.
:include("lib/ident.abnf") ;
:include("lib/numbers.abnf") ;
:include("lib/bools.abnf") ;

:startRule(Program) ;
:whitespace(Whitespace) ;

// PHP whitespace: blanks plus '#' and '//' line comments and '/* */' block comments.
Whitespace  = { @+" \t\r\n" | LineComment | BlockComment } ;
// '#' starts a line comment, but '#[' opens an ATTRIBUTE - the negative lookahead
// keeps the attribute out of the comment. Without it '#[Foo] $p' swallows the rest
// of the line, which is how attributes used to "work" by accident: one written on
// its own line looked like a comment and the declaration under it still parsed,
// while a parameter attribute ate the parameter.
LineComment = ( "//" | HashOnly ) :whitespace() { NotNewline } :whitespace(Whitespace) ;
HashOnly    = "#" !"[" ;
BlockComment = "/*" :whitespace() { NotStar | "*" { "*" } NotStarSlash } "*" { "*" } "/" :whitespace(Whitespace) ;
NotNewline  = "\x00"..."\x09" | "\x0b"..."\U0010ffff" ;
NotStar     = "\x00"..."\x29" | "\x2b"..."\U0010ffff" ;
NotStarSlash = "\x00"..."\x29" | "\x2b"..."\x2e" | "\x30"..."\U0010ffff" ;

// The whole program. A PHP file starts in HTML mode: everything before the first
// '<?php' is literal output, '?>' switches back to HTML mode, and the file may
// alternate any number of times (a file with no '<?php' at all is pure output).
Program     <~~ buildMain(takeAll()) ~~>
            = LeadHtml { TopItem } ;
// One item of a top level: a declaration or a statement. Factored out because a
// BRACED namespace body holds exactly the same items (see NsDecl).
TopItem     = Def <~~ push(makeDefStmt(pop())) ~~> | ClassDecl | EnumDecl | ConstDecl | TypeDecl | NsDecl | UseDecl | Statement ;
OpenTag     = "<?php" :whitespace(Whitespace) ;
// Text before the first open tag. Unlike the '?>' form it keeps a leading newline:
// only the newline directly after a CLOSE tag is swallowed.
LeadHtml    <~~ push(makeHtmlOut(pop(), false)) ~~>
            = HtmlText [ OpenTag ] ;
// '?>' leaves PHP mode. Everything up to the next '<?php' (or end of file) is echoed
// verbatim, and the newline immediately after '?>' is swallowed - PHP's own rule, so
// that a template line does not gain a blank line per tag. It is a STATEMENT, which
// is what makes the interleaved template form work inside a body:
//     <?php foreach ($rows as $r): ?><li><?php echo $r; ?></li><?php endforeach; ?>
// '?>' also stands in for a missing ';' (see Semi), exactly as in PHP.
InlineHtml  <~~ push(makeHtmlOut(pop(), true)) ~~>
            = "?>" HtmlText [ OpenTag ] ;
// The raw run itself. Whitespace skipping is turned OFF around the scan: the leading
// blanks and newlines of the run are OUTPUT, so they must not be eaten as separators
// before the token the scanner returns is matched.
HtmlText    <~~ push(up.in) ~~>
            = :whitespace() HtmlScan :whitespace(Whitespace) ;
HtmlScan = :script(~~
    (function() {
        // Everything from here to the next opening tag is literal text. '<?php' is
        // matched case-insensitively (PHP's tag is), and '<?=' is the echo shorthand,
        // which ShortEcho then parses as an echo statement.
        var end = 0
        var steps = 0
        while (steps < 100000000) {
            steps = steps + 1
            var b = c.peek(end)
            if (b < 0) { break }
            if (b === 60 && c.peek(end + 1) === 63) {
                if (c.peek(end + 2) === 61) { break }
                var p1 = c.peek(end + 2)
                var h1 = c.peek(end + 3)
                var p2 = c.peek(end + 4)
                if ((p1 === 112 || p1 === 80) && (h1 === 104 || h1 === 72) && (p2 === 112 || p2 === 80)) { break }
            }
            end = end + 1
        }
        // An empty run must SUCCEED matching nothing; returning a zero-length token
        // would not. undefined is the dialect's "match empty".
        if (end === 0) { return undefined }
        // c.peek answers BYTES, so the UTF-8 has to be decoded here or a run holding
        // any non-ASCII text would never match back against the source.
        var out = ""
        var p = 0
        while (p < end) {
            var b0 = c.peek(p)
            if (b0 < 128) { out = out + String.fromCharCode(b0); p = p + 1; continue }
            if (b0 >= 240) {
                var cp4 = ((b0 - 240) * 262144) + ((c.peek(p + 1) - 128) * 4096) + ((c.peek(p + 2) - 128) * 64) + (c.peek(p + 3) - 128)
                var off = cp4 - 65536
                out = out + String.fromCharCode(55296 + ((off - (off % 1024)) / 1024)) + String.fromCharCode(56320 + (off % 1024))
                p = p + 4
                continue
            }
            if (b0 >= 224) {
                out = out + String.fromCharCode(((b0 - 224) * 4096) + ((c.peek(p + 1) - 128) * 64) + (c.peek(p + 2) - 128))
                p = p + 3
                continue
            }
            if (b0 >= 192) {
                out = out + String.fromCharCode(((b0 - 192) * 64) + (c.peek(p + 1) - 128))
                p = p + 2
                continue
            }
            return abnf.newToken("\x01badutf8", 0)
        }
        return abnf.newToken(out, 0)
    })()
~~) ;
// '<?= e, e ?>' is shorthand for 'echo e, e;'.
ShortEcho   <~~ push(makeEcho(takeAll())) ~~>
            = ShortOpen <~~ push({cmd: "echo"}) ~~> [ Expression { "," Expression } ] [ ";" ] ;
ShortOpen   = "<?=" :whitespace(Whitespace) ;
// A statement terminator. PHP lets a close tag stand in for the ';' ("echo 1 ?>"),
// so every statement that ends in ';' accepts a '?>' AHEAD of it instead - the tag
// itself is left in place and read as the InlineHtml statement that follows.
Semi        = ";" | CloseAhead ;
CloseAhead  = :script(~~
    (function() {
        var i = 0
        var cc = c.peek(i)
        var steps = 0
        while (steps < 100000) {
            steps = steps + 1
            if (cc === 32 || cc === 9 || cc === 13 || cc === 10) { i = i + 1; cc = c.peek(i); continue }
            break
        }
        if (cc === 63 && c.peek(i + 1) === 62) { return undefined }
        return abnf.newToken("\x01noclosetag", 0)
    })()
~~) ;

// A function definition: function name($a, $b) { ... } with explicit return. An
// optional parameter type hint and return type are accepted and ignored (dynamic).
Def         <~~ push(makeFun(takeAll())) ~~>
            = [ AttrGrp ] KwFunction [ "&" ] Name <~~ push(noteFnName(up.in)) ~~> ParamList [ ReturnType ] Block ;
ParamList   = "(" [ Param { "," Param } [ "," ] ] ")" ;
// A parameter accepts real-world surface: promotion modifiers (public/readonly...),
// a type hint, by-reference '&', variadic '...', and a default value. All but the
// name are accepted and dropped (dynamic, create-on-assign); the default expression
// is parsed then popped so only {param} reaches makeFun.
// A modifier on a constructor parameter is PHP 8's property PROMOTION
// ('__construct(public int $x)' declares and assigns $this->x), so it is captured
// rather than skipped; makeFun turns each promoted parameter into one prologue
// assignment. On any other function a modifier is meaningless and simply ignored.
Param       = [ AttrGrp ] { ParamMod } [ TypeHint ] [ ParamRef ] [ ParamRest ] DollarName <~~ push({param: pop()}) ~~> [ ParamDefault ] ;
// 'function f(&$n)': the argument is passed by REFERENCE. The callee simply keeps the
// cell in its parameter slot (every read dereferences, every write writes through);
// it is the CALL SITE that has to hand a cell over, which is why makeDefStmt records
// each function's by-reference positions for makeCall to read back.
ParamRef    <~~ noteRef(); push({pref: true}) ~~> = "&" ;
// 'function f(...$rest)': every remaining argument collected into one PHP array.
ParamRest   <~~ push({prest: true}) ~~> = "..." ;
ParamMod    <~~ push({pmod: up.in}) ~~> = Modifier ;
// A default value is kept: it becomes a small prologue in the emitted body that
// substitutes the default when the argument was not passed (see paramDefaults).
ParamDefault <~~ push({pdef: pop()}) ~~> = PlainEq Expression ;
TypeHint    = [ "?" ] TypeAtom { ( "|" | "&" ) [ "?" ] TypeAtom } ;
TypeAtom    = KwArray | [ "\\" ] Id { "\\" Id } ;
ReturnType  = ":" TypeHint ;

// class C { public function m() {...} } -- method modifiers are accepted and ignored.
ClassDecl   <~~ push(makeClassStmt(takeAll())) ~~>
            = [ AttrGrp ] { ClassMod } KwClass Name <~~ push(up.in) ~~> [ KwExtends ExtName <~~ push({ext: up.in}) ~~> ] [ KwImplements ImplNames ] "{" { Member } "}" ;
// The implements list of a real class declaration, captured by name (ImplList itself
// stays item-neutral: interface/trait/enum/anonymous-class all reference it and pop a
// fixed number of items).
ImplNames   = ImplName { "," ImplName } ;
ImplName    <~~ push({impl: up.in}) ~~> = ExtName ;
ClassMod    = ( "abstract" | "final" | "readonly" ) KwEnd ;
ExtName     = [ "\\" ] Id { "\\" Id } ;
Member      = MethodDecl | ClassConst | UseTrait | PropDecl ;
// A method: modifiers and a reference-return '&' are accepted and ignored; an
// abstract/interface method has no body (';' instead of a block -> empty method).
MethodDecl  <~~ push({method: makeFun(takeAll())}) ~~>
            = [ AttrGrp ] { Modifier } KwFunction [ "&" ] MethodName <~~ push(noteFnName(up.in)) ~~> ParamList [ ReturnType ] ( Block | ";" ) ;
// Class constants: 'const A = 1, B = 2;', with PHP 8.3's typed form ('const int
// SIZE = 4') and any modifier (final/public/...). They are published on the class
// descriptor under a 'k:' key and resolved by walking __super, so a subclass and an
// implementing class inherit them. An initializer may read 'self::' - makeClassStmt
// binds the class as self BEFORE evaluating them, in declaration order, which is what
// makes 'const TWICE = self::SIZE * 2' work.
ClassConst  <~~ push({cconsts: takeAll()}) ~~>
            = [ AttrGrp ] { Modifier } KwConst CItem { "," CItem } ";" ;
CItem       <~~ push(makeCItem(takeAll())) ~~>
            = CIds [ "=" Expression ] ;
// 'const int SIZE' is a TYPED constant: the last word is the name. Each word pushes
// its own record - up.in of the whole run would concatenate them without a separator
// ("intSIZE"), since the capture drops the skipped whitespace.
CIds        = CWord { CWord } ;
CWord       <~~ push({cname: up.in}) ~~> = Id ;
// use T1, T2 { ... } inside a class body: the members of each trait are FLATTENED
// into the class. The adaptation block resolves the conflicts a flat copy would
// create: 'A::m insteadof B' drops B's m, and 'T::m as alias' adds a second name for
// one (an optional visibility word in front is accepted and ignored).
UseTrait    <~~ push(makeUseTrait(takeAll())) ~~>
            = KwUse ImplNames ( ";" | TraitAdapt ) ;
TraitAdapt  = "{" { TraitRule } "}" ;
TraitRule   = TraitInsteadof | TraitAs ;
TraitInsteadof <~~ push(makeInsteadof(takeAll())) ~~>
            = TraitRef KwInsteadof ImplNames ";" ;
TraitAs     <~~ push(makeTraitAs(takeAll())) ~~>
            = TraitRef KwAs [ TraitVis ] [ TraitAlias ] ";" ;
TraitRef    <~~ push({tref: stripWs(up.in)}) ~~> = Id [ "::" Id ] ;
TraitAlias  <~~ push({alias: up.in}) ~~> = Id ;
TraitVis    <~~ push({tvis: up.in}) ~~> = ( "public" | "private" | "protected" ) KwEnd ;
KwInsteadof = "insteadof" KwEnd ;

// interface I extends J, K { const C = 1; public function m(): T; }
// An interface becomes a class descriptor with no method bodies: it carries its
// constants and its parent interfaces, which is exactly what instanceof, a typed
// catch and 'I::CONST' need. A trait has no runtime model here (its members would
// have to be flattened into every using class) and stays notImplemented.
TypeDecl    = IfaceDecl | TraitDecl ;
IfaceDecl   <~~ push(makeIfaceStmt(takeAll())) ~~>
            = [ AttrGrp ] KwInterface Name <~~ push(up.in) ~~> [ KwExtends ImplNames ] "{" { Member } "}" ;
// trait T { ... }: a compile-time bag of members, with no descriptor of its own -
// PHP traits are a copy/paste unit, not a type - so the declaration emits nothing and
// only records its member list for the classes that 'use' it.
TraitDecl   <~~ push(makeTraitDecl(takeAll())) ~~>
            = [ AttrGrp ] KwTrait Name <~~ push(up.in) ~~> "{" { Member } "}" ;
// namespace N; and the BRACED form namespace N { ... } (and the global 'namespace {}').
// Names are not qualified in this subset, so the declaration itself stays a no-op -
// but the braced body is a top level of its own and its items are simply emitted in
// place, which is what makes a file that wraps everything in 'namespace X { }' work.
NsDecl      <~~ push(makeNs(takeAll(), up.pos)) ~~>
            = KwNamespace { Id | "\\" } ( Semi | NsBody ) ;
NsBody      <~~ push({nsbody: makeBody(takeAll())}) ~~>
            = "{" { TopItem } "}" ;
UseDecl     <~~ push(notImplStmt("use import", up.pos)) ~~>
            = KwUse { Id | "\\" } [ KwAs Id ] Semi ;
// enum Name : backing-type implements I { case A = "a"; const C = 1; method... }
// An enum is a CLASS whose cases are singleton instances published as class constants,
// so 'E::A' is one constant read, 'E::A === E::A' is object identity (which is what
// makes an enum usable as a match arm) and the methods, constants and interfaces are
// the ordinary class machinery. cases() answers them in declaration order.
EnumDecl    <~~ push(makeEnumStmt(takeAll())) ~~>
            = [ AttrGrp ] KwEnum Name <~~ push(up.in) ~~> [ ":" TypeAtom ] [ KwImplements ImplNames ]
              "{" { EnumMember } "}" ;
EnumMember  = EnumCase | Member ;
EnumCase    <~~ push(makeEnumCase(takeAll())) ~~>
            = KwCase Name <~~ push({ecase: up.in}) ~~> [ "=" Expression ] ";" ;
SkipDq      = '"' :whitespace() { SkipStrEsc | SkipDqChar } '"' :whitespace(Whitespace) ;
SkipSq      = "'" :whitespace() { SkipStrEsc | SkipSqChar } "'" :whitespace(Whitespace) ;
SkipStrEsc  = "\\" "\x00"..."\U0010ffff" ;
SkipDqChar  = "\x00"..."\x21" | "\x23"..."\x5b" | "\x5d"..."\U0010ffff" ;
SkipSqChar  = "\x00"..."\x26" | "\x28"..."\x5b" | "\x5d"..."\U0010ffff" ;
// An attribute group '#[Foo(1), Bar]': balanced-skipped wherever PHP allows one.
// Attributes carry no runtime meaning in this subset, so they are recognized and
// dropped rather than routed to notImplemented.
AttrGrp     = Attr { Attr } ;
Attr        = "#[" { AttrItem } "]" ;
AttrItem    = SkipDq | SkipSq | AttrBr | AttrOther ;
AttrBr      = "[" { AttrItem } "]" ;
AttrOther   = "\x00"..."\x21" | "\x23"..."\x26" | "\x28"..."\x5a" | "\x5c" | "\x5e"..."\U0010ffff" ;
// A property declaration 'public $x;' or 'public int $x = e;'. The type hint is
// dropped (dynamic), but the DEFAULT VALUE is kept: makeNew replays the declared
// defaults down the inheritance chain, so "$o->val" reads 7 before anything is
// assigned. A 'static' property is stored on the CLASS descriptor instead (under an
// 's:' key), and because js_phsset walks __super to the declaring class, a subclass
// shares its parent's single storage - PHP's rule.
PropDecl    <~~ push({propdecl: makeProp(takeAll())}) ~~>
            = [ AttrGrp ] { PropMod } [ TypeHint ] DollarName [ "=" Expression ] ( ";" | PropHooks ) ;
// PHP 8.4 property hooks: 'public int $x = 1 { get => e; set { ... } }' (and the
// bodyless 'get;' / 'set;' of an abstract or interface property, which declares a hook
// without providing one). Each hook becomes an ordinary METHOD on the class -
// __hget_<prop> / __hset_<prop> - and a read or write of that property name dispatches
// to it, the same shape __get / __set already use. The property itself stays a
// declared property and IS the backing store: inside its own hook '$this->x' reads and
// writes that store directly instead of re-entering the hook, which is PHP's rule and
// the only thing that keeps a backed hook from recursing forever.
PropHooks   <~~ push({hooks: takeAll()}) ~~>
            = "{" { PropHook } "}" ;
PropHook    <~~ push(makeHook(takeAll())) ~~>
            = [ AttrGrp ] { PropMod } [ "&" ] HookName <~~ push({hname: stripWs(up.in)}) ~~>
              ( HookArrow | HookBlock | ";" ) ;
HookName    = ( "get" | "set" ) KwEnd ;
HookArrow   <~~ push({harrow: pop()}) ~~> = [ ParamList ] "=>" Expression ";" ;
HookBlock   <~~ push({hblock: pop().body}) ~~> = [ ParamList ] Block ;

PropMod     <~~ push({mod: up.in}) ~~> = Modifier ;
// 'private(set)' / 'protected(set)' is PHP 8.4 asymmetric visibility: the setter is
// more restricted than the getter. Visibility is not enforced in this subset, so the
// '(set)' suffix is simply accepted.
Modifier    = ( "public" | "private" | "protected" | "static" | "final" | "abstract" | "readonly" ) KwEnd [ SetVis ] ;
SetVis      = "(" "set" ")" ;

// Body of a control structure / function: braces around a statement sequence.
Block       <~~ push({body: makeBody(takeAll())}) ~~>
            = "{" { Statement } "}" ;

// Every statement pushes exactly one thunk: function(block) -> block.
// A declaration is a statement too: PHP allows `if (...) { class Foo {} }`, and a
// conditionally declared class is common enough in real files to be worth parsing.
Statement   = InlineHtml | ShortEcho | EchoStmt | GlobalStmt | UnsetStmt | StaticVarStmt | Block | If | While | ForEach | For
            | Switch | DoWhile | Try | Throw | Declare
            | ClassDecl | EnumDecl | TypeDecl | ConstDecl | NsDecl | UseDecl
            | Return | Break | Continue | Goto | Label | Empty | ExprStmt ;
// goto L; / L: -- a jump inside one function body. The label is a real basic block, so
// the jump costs one branch; PHP's rule that a goto may LEAVE a block but never enter
// one falls out of where the label's block is created (makeBody registers the labels
// of its own statement run before it emits any of them).
Goto        <~~ push(makeGoto(popLabel())) ~~>
            = KwGoto Name <~~ push({lbl: up.in}) ~~> Semi ;
Label       <~~ push(makeLabelStmt(popLabel())) ~~>
            = Name <~~ push({lbl: up.in}) ~~> ":" !":" ;
KwGoto      = "goto" KwEnd ;

// ----- widened statement surface -----
// declare() is still recognized structurally and routed to notImplemented (its
// directives change nothing this subset models). switch, do-while and goto below are
// genuinely compiled, as try/catch/finally and throw already were.
// static $n = 0; -- a local whose storage SURVIVES the call. The slot is a reference
// cell in the global scope, created once where the program starts (the initializer is
// a constant expression, so evaluating it there is not observable), and the statement
// binds the local name to that cell - so every read and write inside the function goes
// through the same storage, which is exactly what the reference lowering already does.
StaticVarStmt <~~ push(makeStaticVars(takeAll())) ~~>
            = KwStatic StaticVarItem { "," StaticVarItem } Semi ;
StaticVarItem <~~ push(makeStaticVarItem(takeAll())) ~~>
            = DollarName [ "=" Expression ] ;
// switch (e) { case a: ... case b: ... default: ... } and the colon/endswitch form.
// A case FALLS THROUGH into the next one - the arms are chained blocks and only a
// 'break' leaves them - and the labels are compared with PHP's loose '==', which is
// what makes switch differ from match (that one compares with '===').
Switch      <~~ push(makeSwitch(takeAll())) ~~>
            = KwSwitch "(" Expression ")" ( SwitchBlock | SwitchAlt ) ;
SwitchBlock <~~ push({cases: takeAll()}) ~~> = "{" [ ";" ] { SwitchCase } "}" ;
SwitchAlt   <~~ push({cases: takeAll()}) ~~> = ":" [ ";" ] { SwitchCase } KwEndswitch Semi ;
SwitchCase  <~~ push(makeCase(takeAll())) ~~> = CaseLabel { Statement } ;
CaseLabel   = CaseExpr | DefaultLabel ;
CaseExpr    <~~ push({casecond: pop()}) ~~> = KwCase Expression ( ":" | ";" ) ;
DefaultLabel <~~ push({casedflt: true}) ~~> = KwDefault ( ":" | ";" ) ;
KwCase      = "case" KwEnd ;
// do { ... } while (e); -- the body runs before the condition is ever tested.
DoWhile     <~~ push(makeDoWhile(takeAll())) ~~>
            = KwDo ( Block | BodyStmt ) KwWhile "(" Expression ")" Semi ;
// try/catch/finally + throw are genuinely compiled: throw calls js_throw, each try/catch/
// finally body compiles to its own closure handed to js_try, and a return/break/continue
// leaving a try or catch body becomes a control signal that excDispatch re-issues in the
// enclosing frame. Every catch clause is compiled and the first MATCHING one wins: the
// type list (including the multi-catch A | B) is tested with js_phcatch, and a value no
// clause matches is re-thrown. The catch variable is optional. A PHP Block is
// {body: thunk}, so the marked sub-rules unwrap it (pop().body).
Try         <~~ push(makeTry(takeAll())) ~~>
            = KwTry TryBody { CatchClause } [ FinallyClause ] ;
TryBody     <~~ push({trybody: pop().body}) ~~>
            = Block ;
CatchClause <~~ push(makeCatch(takeAll())) ~~>
            = KwCatch "(" CatchType [ DollarName ] ")" Block <~~ push(pop().body) ~~> ;
FinallyClause <~~ push({finbody: pop().body}) ~~>
            = KwFinally Block ;
Throw       <~~ push(makeThrow(pop())) ~~>
            = KwThrow Expression Semi ;
// declare(strict_types=1); / declare(ticks=1) { ... }. None of PHP's three directives
// changes anything this subset models - there is no type coercion to tighten, no tick
// handler and no alternate source encoding - so the declaration itself is a no-op and
// the BRACED form simply emits its body where it stands. The one visible consequence:
// under strict_types real PHP raises a TypeError for a mismatched argument, and this
// subset does not check parameter types at all, so it accepts the call instead.
Declare     <~~ push(makeDeclare(takeAll())) ~~>
            = KwDeclare "(" DeclDirective { "," DeclDirective } [ "," ] ")" ( Semi | Block ) ;
DeclDirective <~~ takeAll() ~~> = Id "=" Expression ;
// const NAME = expr; -- a global constant. Constants live in the same flat name space
// as classes and functions here (they carry no '$' sigil, so no PHP variable can
// collide with one), which is what lets ConstRef resolve them with a plain scope read.
ConstDecl   <~~ push(makeConstDecl(takeAll())) ~~>
            = KwConst CItem { "," CItem } Semi ;
CatchType   = CatchName { "|" CatchName } ;
CatchName   <~~ push({ctype: lastSeg(stripWs(up.in))}) ~~> = ExtName ;

// echo e, e, e;  /  print e;
EchoStmt    <~~ push(makeEcho(takeAll())) ~~>
            = KwEcho <~~ push({cmd: up.in}) ~~> [ Expression { "," Expression } ] Semi ;

// global $a, $b; -- in this subset function bodies already resolve names through
// the enclosing (global) scope chain, so 'global' is accepted as a no-op.
GlobalStmt  <~~ takeAll(); push(makeConstStmt()) ~~>
            = KwGlobal DollarName { "," DollarName } Semi ;

Empty       <~~ push(makeConstStmt()) ~~>
            = ";" ;

ExprStmt    <~~ push(exprToStmt(pop())) ~~>
            = Expression Semi ;

// Both spellings of every compound statement: braces, and PHP's colon/endXXX form.
// The alternative form is tried FIRST because it is decided by a single ':' - it is
// AltBody that then has to be careful: a bare 'endif' would otherwise parse as a
// constant-reference statement and swallow the terminator, which is why the five
// endXXX words are reserved in NotKw.
If          <~~ push(makeIf(takeAll())) ~~>
            = KwIf "(" Expression ")" ( AltIfTail | Block { ElseIf } [ KwElse Block <~~ push({els: pop().body}) ~~> ] ) ;
ElseIf      <~~ var b = pop(); push({elifCond: pop(), elifBody: b.body}) ~~>
            = KwElseif "(" Expression ")" Block ;
AltIfTail   = ":" AltBody { AltElseIf } [ KwElse ":" AltBody <~~ push({els: pop().body}) ~~> ] KwEndif Semi ;
AltElseIf   <~~ var b2 = pop(); push({elifCond: pop(), elifBody: b2.body}) ~~>
            = KwElseif "(" Expression ")" ":" AltBody ;
// A statement run that ends at the enclosing endXXX / else / elseif keyword. It
// pushes the same {body} record a Block does, so every builder is reused unchanged.
AltBody     <~~ push({body: makeBody(takeAll())}) ~~>
            = { Statement } ;

While       <~~ push(makeWhile(pop().body, pop())) ~~>
            = KwWhile "(" Expression ")" ( AltWhileBody | Block | BodyStmt ) ;
AltWhileBody = ":" AltBody KwEndwhile Semi ;

// for (init; cond; post) { body }. The three clauses are optional and wrapped
// with a kind marker so the builder can tell them apart on the stack.
For         <~~ push(makeFor(takeAll())) ~~>
            = KwFor "(" [ ForInit ] ";" [ ForCond ] ";" [ ForPost ] ")" ( AltForBody | Block | BodyStmt ) ;
AltForBody  = ":" AltBody KwEndfor Semi ;
ForInit     <~~ push({k: "init", t: exprToStmt(pop())}) ~~> = Expression ;
ForCond     <~~ push({k: "cond", t: pop()}) ~~> = Expression ;
ForPost     <~~ push({k: "post", t: exprToStmt(pop())}) ~~> = Expression ;

// foreach ($arr as $v) / foreach ($arr as $k => $v)
ForEach     <~~ push(makeForeach(takeAll())) ~~>
            = KwForeach "(" Expression KwAs ForeachVars ")" ( AltForeachBody | Block | BodyStmt ) ;
AltForeachBody = ":" AltBody KwEndforeach Semi ;
// 'as [$k, $v]' destructures each element; the pattern is the same one list()/[] uses
// in an assignment, so both spellings bind identically.
ForeachVars = DestructPat | ForeachKV ;
ForeachKV   = ForeachV1 [ "=>" ForeachV2 ] ;
ForeachV1   = [ FeRef ] DollarName <~~ push({v1: pop()}) ~~> ;
ForeachV2   = [ FeRef ] DollarName <~~ push({v2: pop()}) ~~> ;
// 'as &$v' aliases the array ELEMENT, so writing $v writes back into the array: the
// loop binds the variable to the element's storage CELL instead of to a copy of its
// value.
FeRef       <~~ noteRef(); push({feref: true}) ~~> = "&" ;

Return      <~~ push(makeReturn(takeAll())) ~~>
            = KwReturn [ Expression ] Semi ;
// 'break 2' / 'continue 2' leave that many enclosing loops (and switches). The level
// is a literal integer in PHP - it has to be known where the jump is emitted - so it
// is read from the token rather than compiled to a value.
Break       <~~ push(makeBreak(takeAll())) ~~>
            = KwBreak [ JumpLevel ] Semi ;
Continue    <~~ push(makeContinue(takeAll())) ~~>
            = KwContinue [ JumpLevel ] Semi ;
JumpLevel   <~~ push({lvl: phpIntLit(up.in)}) ~~>
            = "0"..."9" :whitespace() { "0"..."9" } :whitespace(Whitespace) ;

// Every expression pushes exactly one thunk: function(block) -> {b, v}.
Expression  = RefAssign | DestructAssign | Assignment | YieldExpr | Ternary ;

// yield / yield $v / yield $k => $v / yield from $it. A function whose body contains
// one is a GENERATOR function: calling it does not run the body, it makes a generator
// (js_genfn), and each yield suspends the body on its own goroutine - so these are
// real generators, not a replayed list. A keyed yield hands its key over inside a
// small marker record, since the runtime's yield protocol carries a value only.
YieldExpr   <~~ push(makeYield(takeAll())) ~~>
            = KwYield YieldFrom | KwYield YieldArg | KwYield ;
YieldFrom   <~~ push({yfrom: pop()}) ~~> = KwFrom Expression ;
YieldArg    <~~ push(makeYieldArg(takeAll())) ~~> = Ternary [ "=>" Ternary ] ;
KwYield     = "yield" KwEnd ;
KwFrom      = "from" KwEnd ;

// $b = &$a : both names then share ONE storage CELL, so a write through either name
// is visible through the other. The cell is the same one php-interpreter.abnf uses
// (an object carrying __refcell and the value under "v"); it may sit in a scope slot
// or in an array element, which is what makes $b = &$a and $el = &$a[0] one form.
// RefAmp also arms the reference lowering for the whole program: a file with no '&'
// emits not one dereference (see phDeref).
RefAssign   <~~ push(makeRefAssign(takeAll())) ~~>
            = Target PlainEq RefAmp Target ;
RefAmp      <~~ noteRef() ~~> = "&" !"&" ;

// list($a, $b) = e  /  [$a, $b] = e  -- array destructuring. A slot may be EMPTY
// ([, $second]), KEYED (["y" => $py]) or itself a nested pattern ([[$m, $n], [$o]]).
// Tried before a plain expression so a bracketed target list is not mistaken for an
// array literal; a real array literal still rolls back here because the '=' never
// follows. The shape is the interpreter's, so the two agree slot for slot.
DestructAssign <~~ push(makeDestructure(takeAll())) ~~>
            = DestructPat PlainEq Expression ;
DestructPat <~~ push({pat: makePattern(takeAll())}) ~~>
            = KwList "(" DSlots ")" | "[" DSlots "]" ;
DSlots      = DSlot { "," DSlot } ;
// Always pushes exactly one slot record, so an omitted element still occupies its
// position (that is what makes [, $second] bind the SECOND element).
DSlot       <~~ push(makeSlot(takeAll())) ~~>
            = [ DElem ] ;
DElem       = DKeyed | DPlain ;
DKeyed      <~~ var dv = pop(); push({dkey: pop(), dval: dv}) ~~>
            = Expression "=>" DValue ;
DPlain      <~~ push({dval: pop()}) ~~>
            = DValue ;
DValue      = DestructPat | DTarget ;
DTarget     <~~ push({dt: pop()}) ~~>
            = Target ;

// cond ? a : b  /  cond ?: b (Elvis, yields cond when truthy) ; '??' null-coalescing
// binds tighter than the ternary but looser than ||.
Ternary     <~~ push(makeTernary(takeAll())) ~~>
            = Coalesce [ Elvis | "?" Expression ":" Expression ] ;
Elvis       <~~ push({elvis: pop()}) ~~>
            = "?:" Expression ;
Coalesce    <~~ push(makeCoalesce(takeAll())) ~~>
            = OrExpr { "??" OrExpr } ;

Assignment  <~~ push(makeAssign(takeAll())) ~~>
            = Target AssignOp <~~ push(up.in) ~~> Expression ;
// Longest first (ordered choice): '**=' before '*=', '<<=' before '<='. The plain
// '=' declines '==' (a comparison) and '=>' (an array key), neither of which is an
// assignment - without the lookaheads it matches their first character and the rest
// of the sequence then fails somewhere else entirely.
AssignOp    = "**=" | "<<=" | ">>=" | "??=" | "+=" | "-=" | "*=" | "/=" | "%=" | ".="
            | "&=" | "|=" | "^=" | PlainEq ;
PlainEq     = "=" !"=" !">" ;
Target      <~~ push(makeTargetRef(takeAll())) ~~>
            = TargetHead { ArrowField | TIndex | TAppend } ;
// A static property (self::$made = ..., C::$made++) and a variable variable
// ($$name = e, ${expr} = e) are assignable heads too. Neither has a runtime model
// here, so both are recognized and routed to notImplemented; each pushes the same
// {var} record a plain variable does, so makeTargetRef is unchanged. The static form
// must be tried FIRST or PlainHead never sees it.
TargetHead  = StaticHead | PlainHead | VarVarHead ;
// self::$made = e / C::$made++ : a static property as an assignment target. The head
// carries the base thunk and the property name; makeAssign and incDec route those to
// js_phsget / js_phsset instead of the scope.
StaticHead  <~~ push(makeStaticHead(takeAll())) ~~>
            = StaticBaseRef "::" StaticMember ;
// A single-statement body ('while (...) $i++;', 'foreach (...) ;'). It pushes the
// same {body} record a Block does, so every loop builder is reused unchanged.
BodyStmt    <~~ push({body: makeBody(takeAll())}) ~~>
            = Statement ;
PlainHead   = DollarName <~~ push({var: pop()}) ~~> ;
// $$name / ${expr}: the VARIABLE NAME is computed at run time. The head carries the
// name thunk instead of a literal name, and every reader below asks refNameV for the
// name handle - so the scope call is the same one a plain variable makes, with a
// computed string in place of the constant.
VarVarHead  <~~ push({varexpr: pop()}) ~~>
            = "$" ( VarRef | "{" Expression "}" ) ;
ArrowField  = "->" MethodName <~~ push({key: up.in}) ~~> ;
TIndex      <~~ push({idx: pop()}) ~~>
            = "[" Expression "]" ;
TAppend     <~~ push({append: true}) ~~>
            = "[" "]" ;

OrExpr      <~~ push(makeOrAnd(takeAll(), true)) ~~>
            = AndExpr { OrOp AndExpr } ;
OrOp        = "||" | ( "or" KwEnd ) ;
AndExpr     <~~ push(makeOrAnd(takeAll(), false)) ~~>
            = BitOrExpr { AndOp BitOrExpr } ;
AndOp       = "&&" | ( "and" KwEnd ) ;

// The binary operator ladder, in PHP's own precedence order (loosest first):
//   ||  &&  |  ^  &  == != === !== <=>  < <= > >=  .  << >>  + -  * / %  **
// Note that since PHP 8 the concatenation '.' binds LOOSER than the shifts and the
// additive operators, which is why it has its own level rather than sitting next
// to '+'. The single-character bitwise operators decline their doubled forms
// ('&' !'&'), or 'a && b' parses as 'a & (&b)' - a wrong tree, not an error.
// Mirrors the same ladder in php-interpreter.abnf.
BitOrExpr   <~~ push(foldBinary(takeAll())) ~~>
            = BitXorExpr { SingleBar <~~ push(up.in) ~~> BitXorExpr } ;
SingleBar   = "|" !"|" ;
BitXorExpr  <~~ push(foldBinary(takeAll())) ~~>
            = BitAndExpr { "^" <~~ push(up.in) ~~> BitAndExpr } ;
BitAndExpr  <~~ push(foldBinary(takeAll())) ~~>
            = EqExpr { SingleAmp <~~ push(up.in) ~~> EqExpr } ;
SingleAmp   = "&" !"&" ;
EqExpr      <~~ push(foldBinary(takeAll())) ~~>
            = RelExpr { ( "===" | "!==" | "==" | "!=" | "<=>" | "<>" ) <~~ push(up.in) ~~> RelExpr } ;
RelExpr     <~~ push(foldBinary(takeAll())) ~~>
            = ConcatExpr { RelOp <~~ push(up.in) ~~> ConcatExpr } ;
RelOp       = LeNotSpaceship | ">=" | SingleLt | SingleGt ;
LeNotSpaceship = "<=" !">" ;
SingleLt    = "<" !"<" ;
SingleGt    = ">" !">" ;
ConcatExpr  <~~ push(foldBinary(takeAll())) ~~>
            = ShiftExpr { DotOp <~~ push(up.in) ~~> ShiftExpr } ;
DotOp       = "." !"=" ;
ShiftExpr   <~~ push(foldBinary(takeAll())) ~~>
            = AddExpr { ( "<<" | ">>" ) <~~ push(up.in) ~~> AddExpr } ;
AddExpr     <~~ push(foldBinary(takeAll())) ~~>
            = MulExpr { ( "+" | "-" ) <~~ push(up.in) ~~> MulExpr } ;
MulExpr     <~~ push(foldBinary(takeAll())) ~~>
            = UnaryExpr { ( "*" | "/" | "%" ) <~~ push(up.in) ~~> UnaryExpr } ;

UnaryExpr   = Cast | Clone | InstanceofExpr | PreIncDec | Neg | Pos | BitNot | NotOp
            | ThrowExpr | PowExpr ;
NotOp       <~~ push(makeNot(pop())) ~~>
            = "!" UnaryExpr ;
BitNot      <~~ push(makeBitNot(pop())) ~~>
            = "~" UnaryExpr ;
// '**' is RIGHT associative (2 ** 3 ** 2 is 512, not 64) and binds tighter than
// every prefix operator on its left but looser than the one on its right, so its
// right operand is a full UnaryExpr ('2 ** -1').
PowExpr     <~~ push(foldPow(takeAll())) ~~>
            = PostfixExpr [ "**" UnaryExpr ] ;
// throw is an EXPRESSION since PHP 8 ($x ?? throw new E, fn() => throw new E).
ThrowExpr   <~~ push(makeThrowExpr(pop())) ~~>
            = KwThrow Expression ;
// (int)$x / (string)$y / (array)$z ...: a type cast, lowered to js_phcast - the same
// conversion table php-interpreter.abnf's phpCast has, so (int)3.9 is 3, (float)5 is
// the boxed 5.0 and (array)5 is [5]. A parenthesized group '($x)' is not a cast (the
// inner is not a cast keyword) and falls through to Primary.
Cast        <~~ push(makeCast(takeAll())) ~~>
            = "(" CastKw ")" UnaryExpr ;
CastKw      <~~ push({cast: stripWs(up.in)}) ~~>
            = ( "int" | "integer" | "bool" | "boolean" | "float" | "double" | "real"
              | "string" | "array" | "object" | "unset" | "binary" ) KwEnd ;
// clone $o : a copy of the instance whose __clone hook, when the class has one,
// runs on the COPY. Array properties are values, so they are copied along.
Clone       <~~ push(makeClone(pop())) ~~>
            = KwClone UnaryExpr ;
// $x instanceof Foo : the runtime type test (js_phisa), which matches the class
// itself, every base class and every implemented interface. Tried before the plain
// unary path; if no 'instanceof' follows, this alternative fails and rolls back, so an
// ordinary unary keeps its exact original parse.
InstanceofExpr <~~ push(makeInstanceof(takeAll())) ~~>
            = PostfixExpr KwInstanceof InstRef ;
InstRef     = InstDyn | InstName ;
InstDyn     <~~ push({idyn: makeVarRef(pop())}) ~~> = DollarName ;
InstName    <~~ push({iname: lastSeg(stripWs(up.in))}) ~~> = ExtName ;
PreIncDec   <~~ push(makePreIncDec(pop(), pop())) ~~>
            = ( "++" | "--" ) <~~ push(up.in) ~~> Target ;
Neg         <~~ push(makeNeg(pop())) ~~>
            = "-" UnaryExpr ;
Pos         = "+" UnaryExpr ;

PostfixExpr = IncDec | CallMember ;
IncDec      <~~ push(makeIncDec(takeAll())) ~~>
            = Target ( "++" | "--" ) <~~ push(up.in) ~~> ;

// A primary followed by any mix of ->method(args), ->field, [index], (call), ::static.
CallMember  <~~ push(foldCallMember(takeAll())) ~~>
            = Primary { MethodSfx | DynMethodSfx | FieldSfx | DynFieldSfx | IndexSfx | CallSfx | StaticSfx } ;
// '->' and the NULLSAFE '?->'. A '?->' whose receiver is null makes the WHOLE chain
// yield null without evaluating anything to its right - including the arguments of
// the call it introduces - which foldCallMember implements as a branch to the chain's
// end block (PHP's short circuit).
Arrow       = NsArrow | "->" ;
NsArrow     <~~ push({ns: true}) ~~> = "?->" ;
MethodSfx   <~~ push(makeMethodSfx(takeAll())) ~~>
            = Arrow MethodName <~~ push({mname: up.in}) ~~> "(" [ ArgList ] ")" ;
// $o->$meth(...) / $o->{"add"}(...) / $o->$prop: the member name is computed at run
// time. There is no dynamic-member dispatch here, so both forms are recognized and
// routed to notImplemented; each still pushes exactly one chain suffix.
DynMethodSfx <~~ push(makeDynMethodSfx(takeAll())) ~~>
            = Arrow DynName "(" [ ArgList ] ")" ;
DynName     <~~ push({dynname: pop()}) ~~> = VarRef | "{" Expression "}" ;
DynFieldSfx <~~ push(makeDynFieldSfx(takeAll())) ~~>
            = Arrow DynName ;
FieldSfx    <~~ push(makeFieldSfx(takeAll())) ~~>
            = Arrow MethodName <~~ push({fname: up.in}) ~~> ;
IndexSfx    <~~ push({kind: "index", idx: pop()}) ~~>
            = "[" Expression "]" ;
CallSfx     <~~ push({kind: "call", args: takeAll()}) ~~>
            = "(" [ ArgList ] ")" ;
// An argument list: positional, named (h: 5), spread (...$a) or the PHP 8.1
// first-class callable placeholder (strlen(...)), with an optional trailing comma.
// Each form pushes exactly ONE record, and emitPhArgs turns the run into the
// positional list the runtime calls with - resolving a named argument against the
// callee's parameter names, merging a spread (whose STRING keys are named arguments
// of their own, which is what `f(...["w" => 1])` means) and handing a storage cell
// over for a by-reference parameter.
// PosArg also records its own source text: a call site can only pass a REFERENCE for
// an argument that denotes one, and '$name' is exactly the spelling that does.
ArgList     = Arg { "," Arg } [ "," ] ;
Arg         = NamedArg | SpreadArg | FccArg | PosArg ;
NamedArg    <~~ push({aname: argLabel(up.in), pos: pop()}) ~~>
            = ArgLabel Expression ;
ArgLabel    = Id ":" !":" ;
SpreadArg   <~~ push({aspread: pop()}) ~~>
            = "..." Expression ;
FccArg      <~~ push({fcc: true}) ~~>
            = "..." ;
PosArg      <~~ push({pos: pop(), vtext: plainVarText(up.in)}) ~~>
            = Expression ;
// The same access as a chain SUFFIX ($obj::CONST, $obj::class, $obj::method()).
StaticSfx   <~~ push(makeStaticSfx(takeAll())) ~~>
            = "::" StaticMember [ StaticArgs ] ;

Primary     = "(" Expression ")"
            | IssetExpr | EmptyExpr
            | NewAnon | NewExpr | ClosureExpr | ArrowFn | Match | ArrayLit
            | IncludeExpr
            | HeredocLit | DqStr | SqStr | NumLit
            | True | False | Null
            | VarRef | VarVarRef | StaticExpr | Call | ConstRef ;

// include / require (and _once): a file-inclusion expression. A single-quoted string
// literal operand ('geomlib.php') names a project file: its path is resolved and the
// file's top-level function / class declarations are prepended to phpmain (resolveImport
// / core.importFile, lib/compile-core.js). Any other operand - a dynamic expression, or a
// double-quoted string - is parsed and routed to notImplemented, exactly as before.
IncludeExpr <~~ push(makeInclude(takeAll(), up.pos)) ~~>
            = ( "include_once" | "include" | "require_once" | "require" ) KwEnd ( IncPath | IncDyn ) ;
// A single-quoted literal path, captured raw like SqStr but kept as the import path
// string (via up.in) rather than lowered to a runtime value.
IncPath     = "'" :whitespace() { SqEsc | SqPlain } <~~ push({incpath: unescapeSq(up.in)}) ~~> "'" :whitespace(Whitespace) ;
IncDyn      <~~ push({incdyn: pop()}) ~~>
            = Expression ;
// A bareword constant reference: a global/class constant or magic constant (PHP_EOL,
// M_PI, __DIR__, __LINE__, ...). No constant model here, so it is routed to
// notImplemented. Placed last so calls, statics and keywords are matched first.
// A namespace-qualified name in expression position: \PHP_EOL, Foo\Bar::baz(),
// new \ReflectionClass(...). Namespaces have no runtime model here (NsDecl and
// UseDecl are notImplemented), so a qualified name resolves on its LAST segment,
// which is what makes the common `\Exception` / `\strlen` spelling work.
QName       = [ "\\" ] Name { "\\" MethodName } ;
ConstRef    <~~ push(makeConstRef(lastSeg(stripWs(up.in)))) ~~>
            = QName ;

// new C(...) / new static(...) / new $cn(...) / new ($expr)(...). The class may be
// named, RELATIVE (self/static/parent - `new static()` is what a late-static-binding
// factory returns) or computed at run time from a string or a descriptor. Declared
// property defaults are replayed by the class's own __initprops hook, so the dynamic
// forms get them too.
// isset($a, $b["k"], $o->p): true when every operand is set AND not null. It is a
// language construct, not a function - the operands are storage LOCATIONS, so a
// missing variable or a missing array key answers false instead of aborting.
// empty(e) is the negation of PHP truthiness, and unset() removes the binding.
IssetExpr   <~~ push(makeIsset(takeAll())) ~~>
            = KwIsset "(" Target { "," Target } [ "," ] ")" ;
EmptyExpr   <~~ push(makeEmpty(pop())) ~~>
            = KwEmpty "(" Expression ")" ;
UnsetStmt   <~~ push(makeUnset(takeAll())) ~~>
            = KwUnset "(" Target { "," Target } [ "," ] ")" Semi ;
KwIsset     = "isset" KwEnd ;
KwEmpty     = "empty" KwEnd ;
KwUnset     = "unset" KwEnd ;

NewExpr     <~~ push(makeNew(takeAll())) ~~>
            = KwNew NewTarget [ "(" [ ArgList ] ")" ] ;
NewTarget   = NewRel | NewDyn | NewParen | NewName ;
NewRel      <~~ push({crel: stripWs(up.in)}) ~~> = ( "static" | "self" | "parent" ) KwEnd ;
NewDyn      <~~ push({cdyn: pop()}) ~~> = VarRef ;
NewParen    <~~ push({cdyn: pop()}) ~~> = "(" Expression ")" ;
NewName     <~~ push({cls: lastSeg(stripWs(up.in))}) ~~> = QName ;
// new class (args) extends B implements I { ... }: an anonymous class. The body is
// the ORDINARY class body, so it produces exactly the member records a named
// declaration does and makeClassStmt builds the descriptor unchanged - the only
// difference is that the declaration is emitted where the expression stands, under a
// generated name, and the instantiation follows it.
NewAnon     <~~ push(makeNewAnon(takeAll())) ~~>
            = KwNew KwClass [ "(" [ ArgList ] ")" ] [ KwExtends ExtName <~~ push({ext: up.in}) ~~> ]
              [ KwImplements ImplNames ] "{" { Member } "}" ;

// fn (params) => expr : an arrow function. It is a closure whose body is a single
// expression that is RETURNED, and whose capture is automatic - which is exactly what
// this compiler's closures already do (they close over the whole defining scope), so
// it lowers to the same js_closure over an emitted function with a synthesized
// 'return expr' body. The body must be marked: ParamList pushes {param} records and
// the body pushes a bare thunk, and makeArrowFn has to tell them apart.
ArrowFn     <~~ push(makeArrowFn(takeAll())) ~~>
            = [ AttrGrp ] [ "static" KwEnd ] KwFn [ "&" ] ParamList [ ReturnType ] "=>" ArrowBody ;
ArrowBody   <~~ push({arrowbody: pop()}) ~~>
            = Expression ;
// match (subject) { c1, c2 => e, default => e } : PHP 8's match EXPRESSION. Each arm
// compares with '===' (js_phseq), which is why it needed the boxed float first:
// match (3.0) tells 3 and 3.0 apart. Arms are tried in order and the first hit wins;
// with no arm and no default PHP raises UnhandledMatchError - this lowering yields
// null instead, the one documented deviation.
Match       <~~ push(makeMatch(takeAll())) ~~>
            = KwMatch "(" Expression ")" "{" [ MatchArm { "," MatchArm } [ "," ] ] "}" ;
MatchArm    <~~ push(makeArm(takeAll())) ~~>
            = ( MatchDflt | MatchConds ) "=>" MatchBody ;
MatchDflt   <~~ push({mdflt: true}) ~~> = KwDefault ;
MatchConds  = MatchCond { "," MatchCond } ;
MatchCond   <~~ push({mcond: pop()}) ~~> = Expression ;
MatchBody   <~~ push({mbody: pop()}) ~~> = Expression ;
KwDefault   = "default" KwEnd ;

// Class::CONST / Class::method() / Class::$prop / Class::class, and the three
// relative bases. 'self' is the class the method was DECLARED in, 'static' is the
// late-static-binding class and 'parent' is self's __super. self and static live in
// the CALL FRAME (the hidden "self" / "static" scope entries emitFunc declares), not
// in globals, so the scope-chain snapshot makeTry restores covers them and an
// exception can never leave them pointing at the wrong class.
StaticExpr  <~~ push(makeStaticExpr(takeAll())) ~~>
            = StaticBaseRef "::" StaticMember [ StaticArgs ] ;
StaticBaseRef <~~ push({sbase: stripWs(up.in)}) ~~>
            = ( "static" KwEnd | QName ) ;
StaticMember <~~ push({smem: up.in}) ~~>
            = MethodName | DollarName ;
StaticArgs  <~~ push({sargs: takeAll()}) ~~>
            = "(" [ ArgList ] ")" ;

// A closure: function($a) use ($b, $c) { ... } (optionally 'static'). The use list is
// captured lexically (this subset captures the whole defining scope).
ClosureExpr <~~ push(makeClosure(takeAll())) ~~>
            = [ AttrGrp ] [ "static" KwEnd ] KwFunction ParamList [ UseClause ] [ ReturnType ] Block ;
UseClause   = KwUse "(" [ UseVar { "," UseVar } ] [ "," ] ")" ;
// 'use ($x)' captures by VALUE - a snapshot taken where the closure is created, so a
// later write to $x outside is not visible inside. 'use (&$x)' captures by REFERENCE,
// which needs no snapshot at all: a closure here already closes over the defining
// scope, so an unlisted name and a by-reference one both resolve to the live variable.
UseVar      = [ UseRef ] DollarName <~~ push({use: pop()}) ~~> ;
UseRef      <~~ push({useref: true}) ~~> = "&" ;

// [1, 2] / [k => v] / array(1, 2). '=>' entries make an associative array
// (ordered map); plain entries make an indexed array.
ArrayLit    <~~ push(makeArray(takeAll())) ~~>
            = "[" [ ArrayItem { "," ArrayItem } [ "," ] ] "]"
            | KwArray "(" [ ArrayItem { "," ArrayItem } [ "," ] ] ")" ;
ArrayItem   = SpreadItem | KeyedItem | PlainItem ;
// [...$a] merges another array in (integer keys renumber, string keys are kept);
